the Warsaw Ghetto Uprising was the  largest single revolt by Jews during  World War two Nazi Germany cleared the  Jewish quarter of the city with brutal  force thousands were killed and anyone  who survived was taken to a  concentration camp several hundred  children were smuggled out of the ghetto  by the Polish resistance and survived  our reporter met two of them in Warsaw  yana sobolevskaya piece was four years  old when she was rescued from the warsaw  ghetto she was saved by Polish  resistance fighters it would be years  before she discovered how lucky she'd  been this is me this is her earliest  photo taken after the occupation her  Jewish parents had her smuggled out of  the ghetto something she no longer  remembers my father was called Green  spawn and my mother Zilber but how did  you discover what had happened to you  I found family members in Israel after I  first heard the old family names  I wrote to an Israeli newspaper and  that's how I found an uncle one day  prior to the ghetto uprising Sobolev  capice was brought to a Polish  resistance fighter here in Warsaw a  woman who found adoptive parents for  Sobolev Skippy's her new parents had her  baptized as Catholic it wasn't until her  18th birthday that she found out about  her Jewish origins wanting to learn more  about her parents  so that obscure piece contacted the  former resistance fighter she told me  you have to be grateful to the sober  left skis for raising you and for  allowing you to study she stressed the  role the sober left skis played but I  wanted to find out as much as possible  about my biological family this wasn't  easy  after the ghetto uprising in 1943 the  Nazis had nearly all remaining Jewish  survivors deported to concentration  camps almost all were murdered  today a monument commemorating the  ghetto and its 300,000 killed  inhabitants marks the location where the  infamous footbridge once connected the  large and small quarter here Jewish  people lived densely packed the German  occupiers didn't allow them to exit the  ghetto without permission and yet Polish  resistance fighters managed to smuggle  Jewish children out past the guards the  fate of these children is being  researched by the Poland Museum of the  history of Polish Jews in Warsaw  including the fate of Christina but  Natsuko when the ghetto uprising began  in 1943 but Natsuko was still inside it  she is one of its last surviving  witnesses and where was the bunker in  which you hid Christina it was on the  corner of salmon hopper Street and milla  Street it's always been hard for me to  get more information  I try to find out who on the so-called  Aryan side helped me to this day I don't  know which resistance fighters saved me  cause it's a gotta the secret council  for aid to Jews was it the Jewish  Committee I still don't know but Natsuko  was 11 years old when her siblings hid  her in a bunker that had been  constructed under the house at the time  two of her brothers had already been  deported and killed in the Treblinka  death camp  when the uprising began she went even  deeper to hide herself down into the  sewers while German occupying forces  attacked the Jewish resistance above  with flamethrowers and gas grenades but  'not scre now 85 stayed hidden below  with three others until she was saved by  resistance fighters we were brought  outside through sewage pipes and then  put into sacks why into sacks we don't  look human anymore we were just skin and  bones  we had no strength left  we had been underground for nine months  without light and without contact to the  outside world but she opted where is  this contact she was kept hidden until  the end of the war and then brought to  an orphanage today  Bhootnath seeker tells young people her  story to warn of right-wing extremism if  this big group goes into Warsaw and  bears witness to what I've told you then  I don't think anti-semitism can survive  it's in your hands I wish you well thank  you  Krystyna botnets car and yana  sobolevskaya piece are founding members  of the children of the Holocaust  Association they want to share their  story about life and death in the Warsaw  Ghetto as often as they can
 

